Sets	p5-3D.txt	/^the position and orientation of the current camera in a 3D sketch.$/
It	p5-3D.txt	/^has nine parameters that are all optional.$/
The	p5-3D.txt	/^first three parameters, `x`, `y`, and `z`, are the coordinates of the camera’s position.$/
For	p5-3D.txt	/^example, calling `camera(0, 0, 0)` places the camera at the origin `(0, 0, 0)`.$/
By	p5-3D.txt	/^default, the camera is placed at `(0, 0, 800)`.$/
The	p5-3D.txt	/^next three parameters, ``centerX``, ``centerY``, and `centerZ` are the coordinates of the `point` where the camera faces.$/
For	p5-3D.txt	/^example, calling `camera(0, 0, 0, 10, 20, 30)` places the camera at the origin `(0, 0, 0)` and points it at `(10, 20, 30)`.$/
By	p5-3D.txt	/^default, the camera points at the origin `(0, 0, 0)`.$/
The	p5-3D.txt	/^last three parameters, `upX`, `upY`, and `upZ` are the components of the "up" vector.$/
The	p5-3D.txt	/^"up" vector orients the camera’s y-axis.$/
For	p5-3D.txt	/^example, calling `camera(0, 0, 0, 10, 20, 30, 0, -1, 0)` places the camera at the origin `(0, 0, 0)`, points it at `(10, 20, 30)`, and sets the "up" vector to `(0, -1, 0)` which is like holding it upside-down.$/
By	p5-3D.txt	/^default, the "up" vector is `(0, 1, 0)`.$/
See	p5-3D.txt	/^also: ~$/
Sets	p5-3D.txt	/^a perspective projection for the camera.$/
In	p5-3D.txt	/^a perspective projection, shapes that are further from the camera appear smaller than shapes that are near the camera.$/
This	p5-3D.txt	/^technique, called foreshortening, creates realistic 3D scenes.$/
The	p5-3D.txt	/^frustum is the volume of space that’s visible to the camera.$/
The	p5-3D.txt	/^frustum’s shape is a pyramid with its top cut off.$/
The	p5-3D.txt	/^camera is placed where the top of the pyramid should be and points towards the base of the pyramid.$/
It	p5-3D.txt	/^views everything within the frustum.$/
The	p5-3D.txt	/^first parameter, `fovy`, is the camera’s vertical field of view.$/
For	p5-3D.txt	/^example, calling `myCamera.perspective(0.5)` sets the camera’s vertical field of view to 0.5 radians.$/
By	p5-3D.txt	/^default, `fovy` is calculated based on the sketch’s `height` and the camera’s default z-coordinate, which is 800.$/
The	p5-3D.txt	/^formula for the default `fovy` is `2 * atan(`height` \/ 2 \/ 800)`.$/
The	p5-3D.txt	/^second parameter, `aspect`, is the camera’s aspect ratio.$/
For	p5-3D.txt	/^example, calling `myCamera.perspective(0.5, 1.5)` sets the camera’s field of view to 0.5 radians and aspect ratio to 1.5, which would make shapes appear thinner on a square canvas.$/
By	p5-3D.txt	/^default, `aspect` is `set` to ``width` \/ `height``.$/
The	p5-3D.txt	/^third parameter, `near`, is the distance from the camera to the near plane.$/
For	p5-3D.txt	/^example, calling `myCamera.perspective(0.5, 1.5, 100)` sets the camera’s field of view to 0.5 radians, its aspect ratio to 1.5, and places the near plane 100 `pixels` from the camera.$/
Any	p5-3D.txt	/^shapes drawn less than 100 `pixels` from the camera won’t be visible.$/
By	p5-3D.txt	/^default, `near` is `set` to `0.1 * 800`, which is 1\/10th the default distance between the camera and the origin.$/
The	p5-3D.txt	/^fourth parameter, `far`, is the distance from the camera to the far plane.$/
For	p5-3D.txt	/^example, calling `myCamera.perspective(0.5, 1.5, 100, 10000)` sets the camera’s field of view to 0.5 radians, its aspect ratio to 1.5, places the near plane 100 `pixels` from the camera, and places the far plane 10,000 `pixels` from the camera.$/
Any	p5-3D.txt	/^shapes drawn more than 10,000 `pixels` from the camera won’t be visible.$/
By	p5-3D.txt	/^default, `far` is `set` to `10 * 800`, which is 10 times the default distance between the camera and the origin.$/
